WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.667
Hi, I'm Quire!

00:00:02.667 --> 00:00:06.493
Today: how training moves its last per-layer tensor off the GPU, and

00:00:06.493 --> 00:00:08.407
why the copy can be free.

00:00:10.142 --> 00:00:12.229
Checkpointing keeps only each layer's input.

00:00:12.229 --> 00:00:15.013
That input sits idle until backward reaches it.

00:00:15.013 --> 00:00:17.796
So it can wait in host memory instead.

00:00:19.592 --> 00:00:24.792
In forward, a checkpointed layer saves just one tensor: its input, the residual stream.

00:00:25.212 --> 00:00:28.531
A hook copies that tensor to pinned host memory, and the

00:00:28.531 --> 00:00:29.737
GPU copy is freed.

00:00:30.157 --> 00:00:33.914
In backward, it is copied back, ideally one layer early, so the

00:00:33.914 --> 00:00:35.166
recompute finds it waiting.

00:00:36.608 --> 00:00:39.426
Transformers wraps each checkpoint in save on CPU.

00:00:39.426 --> 00:00:44.005
Its copies run on the compute stream, so the GPU waits for them.

00:00:44.425 --> 00:00:48.450
Arctic copies on two side streams and prefetches the previous layer, so

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:50.127
the copies run under compute.

00:00:51.608 --> 00:00:52.732
Here is the formula.

00:00:53.608 --> 00:00:57.128
One checkpoint is batch times sequence times hidden, two bytes each.

00:00:57.548 --> 00:00:59.457
Divide by the host link's bandwidth.

00:00:59.457 --> 00:01:02.957
If that is shorter than the layer's compute, overlap hides it.

00:01:04.342 --> 00:01:08.631
Worked out for an eight billion parameter model, the synchronous cost shrinks

00:01:08.631 --> 00:01:12.563
as sequences grow, because attention compute grows faster than the copy.

00:01:13.883 --> 00:01:17.035
Without offload, memory climbs with every layer in forward.

00:01:17.035 --> 00:01:17.735
A hill.

00:01:18.155 --> 00:01:21.333
With checkpoint offload to CPU, the hill is gone.

00:01:21.333 --> 00:01:23.099
Only the backward spikes remain.

00:01:24.392 --> 00:01:27.258
The checkpoint is idle between forward and backward.

00:01:27.258 --> 00:01:30.482
Overlap the copies, and moving it costs almost nothing.

00:01:32.317 --> 00:01:33.214
Keep the input.

00:01:33.214 --> 00:01:34.708
Park it on the host.

00:01:34.708 --> 00:01:35.605
Overlap the copies.

00:01:36.917 --> 00:01:39.000
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:39.595
I'm Quire.

00:01:39.595 --> 00:01:39.893
Bye!
